# A Certifiable Multimodal Anomaly Detection and Autonomous Safety Architecture for Liquid Rocket Static Fire Testing: Integrating Hybrid Physics-Informed Digital Twins, Failure-Diverse N-Version Redundancy, and Runtime-Governed Human-AI Teaming

**Ouadi Maakoul**

---

## Abstract

Static fire testing and pre-launch ground operations remain the highest-risk phases of liquid rocket development, historically generating 60–70% of catastrophic test failures through cascading, multi-modal fault modes that evade fixed deterministic thresholds. Contemporary safety architectures, relying on reactive interlocks and human-in-the-loop monitoring, exhibit critical limitations in detecting novel anomalies, quantifying decision uncertainty, and satisfying the certification landscape that—following the EU Digital Omnibus of 2026—now extends to December 2027 for standalone high-risk AI systems and August 2028 for AI embedded in regulated products. This paper proposes a rigorously scoped, certifiable safety architecture that transitions from reactive thresholding to predictive, explainable, and autonomously verifiable safety management across four tightly integrated layers: (1) a **Hybrid Physics-Informed Machine Learning Digital Twin (Hybrid PIML-DT)** with Neumann-boundary-condition heat flux prediction and Bayesian online calibration, targeting sub-50 ms inference latency; (2) an unsupervised multimodal anomaly detection pipeline combining deep variational autoencoders with **Censoring-Aware Extreme Value Theory (CenEVT)** for threshold-free novel fault identification under modality incompleteness and small-sample tail estimation; (3) a **failure-diverse N-version redundant decision architecture** implementing Two-out-of-Three (2oo3) voting with a formally evaluated failure diversity protocol and deterministic hardware fallback; and (4) a **runtime-governed Human-AI Teaming (HAT) protocol** anchored in Explainable AI (XAI), per-action oversight evidence generation, and immutable cryptographic audit logging. The proposed framework is explicitly aligned with the regulatory environment as revised in 2026: FAA 14 CFR Part 450 (fully mandatory from March 10, 2026), the EU AI Act (Regulation EU 2024/1689, with high-risk AI provisions for Annex III systems deferred to December 2, 2027 by the EU Digital Omnibus, Regulation 2026/1744), the NIST AI RMF Trustworthy AI Profile for Critical Infrastructure (April 2026 concept note), ISO 14620-1:2024, ECSS-Q-ST-40C, and EASA NPA 2025-07 on AI trustworthiness, which remains in consultation phase with a second NPA planned for 2026. Validation is structured across three phases—offline benchmarking, hardware-in-the-loop emulation, and sub-scale static fire bench testing—targeting a TRL 5→6 transition by Year 4. The expected outcome is an industry-transferable safety architecture that demonstrably reduces catastrophic test failure rates, lowers false-positive abort rates below 10%, and establishes a replicable certification pathway for AI-augmented propulsion safety.

**Keywords:** Hybrid physics-informed machine learning, digital twin certification, anomaly detection, censoring-aware extreme value theory, N-version redundancy, failure diversity, human-AI teaming, liquid rocket engine, static fire safety, EU AI Act, runtime governance, explainable AI.

