## 1. Introduction

The validation of liquid rocket engines through static fire campaigns is an operationally irreplaceable and inherently hazardous process. Ground test sequences encompassing cryogenic propellant loading, ignition verification, full-thrust combustion monitoring, and autonomous shutdown logic expose test infrastructure, personnel, and vehicle hardware to compound risk profiles that have no reliable analogue in other engineering domains. Historical failure analyses consistently demonstrate that the majority of catastrophic test events originate not from a single sensor exceedance but from cascading, multi-modal fault sequences that propagate faster than deterministic interlock systems can respond and often manifest as previously unobserved fault topologies invisible to threshold-based detectors.

Current industrial practice in propulsion ground safety rests on three pillars that, while mature, are structurally inadequate for the complexity of modern liquid engine test campaigns: fixed-limit safety interlocks derived from worst-case design margins, human operator monitoring whose bandwidth and situational awareness are bounded by cognitive load constraints, and post-failure forensic analysis that yields knowledge too late to prevent the event. These limitations have been articulated across successive NASA, ESA, and ArianeGroup health management programs, most recently in the DiRETHMS, SAFEST, and ENLIGHTEN technical reports, which collectively advocate for edge-deployed continuous learning architectures capable of detecting fault precursors before threshold violation.

The academic and industrial landscape of 2025–2026 presents a convergence of enabling conditions that make a departure from purely reactive safety feasible: hybrid physics-informed machine learning capable of real-time inference at engineering-relevant latency with improved generalization over pure PINN approaches; unsupervised anomaly detection that generalizes to novel fault modes without exhaustive labeled training data, including censoring-aware extreme value theory that operates on small samples; edge computing platforms that support deterministic AI inference; and, critically, a maturing regulatory environment that—while deferring some deadlines—now actively prescribes rather than merely permits AI-assisted safety decision-making in high-consequence aerospace operations.

A critical development during 2026 has been the **EU Digital Omnibus (Regulation 2026/1744)** , which entered into force on July 27, 2026, and deferred the compliance deadline for standalone high-risk AI systems (Annex III, which includes critical infrastructure management) to **December 2, 2027**, and for AI embedded in regulated products to **August 2, 2028**. This deferral—a 16-month extension for Annex III systems—was a direct response to delays in establishing conformity assessment bodies and harmonized technical standards. Far from relaxing safety obligations, this extended timeline provides a strategic preparation window for architectures such as the one proposed here to align with forthcoming standards and to integrate emerging certification frameworks for digital twins and AI systems.

Against this context, the present paper contributes the following. First, we formalize a four-layer certifiable safety architecture specifically scoped to liquid rocket static fire testing, moving beyond fragmented point solutions to an integrated system with well-defined interfaces, arbitration logic, and human oversight protocols. Second, we ground each architectural layer in the most current available literature and validate its design choices against measurable performance targets, incorporating validated solutions to previously identified technical gaps: hybrid PIML for digital twin generalization, Neumann boundary conditions for certifiable heat flux, CenEVT for small-sample tail prediction, failure diversity evaluation for N-version redundancy, and runtime per-action governance for human oversight. Third, we construct a certification pathway explicitly mapped to the 2026 regulatory milieu as revised, including the full-force application of FAA 14 CFR Part 450, the EU AI Act Annex III high-risk AI requirements as deferred to December 2027, the NIST AI RMF Critical Infrastructure Profile, EASA's AI trustworthiness rulemaking task, and emerging digital twin certification architectures. Fourth, we propose a phased validation strategy that advances the architecture from computational demonstration through hardware-in-the-loop emulation to sub-scale engine testing, achieving a documented TRL progression suitable for academic publication and industrial adoption.

The remainder of this paper is structured as follows. Section 2 reviews the state of the art across the four constituent technical domains, including validated solutions to identified gaps. Section 3 formalizes the problem statement and research questions. Section 4 describes the system architecture in detail. Section 5 presents the validation methodology and experimental design. Section 6 addresses the regulatory and certification framework as revised in 2026. Section 7 discusses limitations and future extensions. Section 8 concludes.

