## 3. Problem Statement and Research Questions

**Core Problem:** How can a safety architecture for liquid rocket static fire testing reliably detect both known and novel fault precursors in real-time, quantify decision uncertainty, execute autonomous safety interventions, and satisfy the aerospace certification requirements that are legally binding from 2026–2027, while maintaining meaningful human oversight and empirically validated failure independence?

**RQ1:** How can a Hybrid PIML Digital Twin integrated with Bayesian online calibration and Neumann-boundary-condition heat flux prediction minimize the simulation-to-reality gap sufficiently to enable real-time inference below 50 ms latency for LOX/LCH₄ and LOX/RP-1 static fire conditions, while providing certifiable wall-level thermal margin estimates?

**RQ2:** How can an unsupervised multimodal anomaly detection pipeline integrating deep variational autoencoders with Censoring-Aware Extreme Value Theory detect novel fault modes without predefined thresholds while maintaining detection performance above 85% recall and below 10% false positive rate under conditions of up to two simultaneous sensor dropouts and with limited baseline data?

**RQ3:** What failure-diverse N-version redundant architecture and deterministic arbitration mechanism ensures fail-operational safety, empirically mitigates common-cause failures between AI, physics-based, and rule-based decision paths, and satisfies the verifiability requirements of DO-178C adaptation guidelines and ECSS-Q-ST-40C?

**RQ4:** How can XAI attribution, multimodal cognitive load monitoring, runtime per-action governance, and a graded autonomy protocol be structured to satisfy the EU AI Act Annex III transparency and human oversight requirements as deferred to December 2027, the NIST AI RMF Critical Infrastructure Profile released April 2026, and the EASA NPA 2025-07 AI trustworthiness framework in its evolving consultation-phase form?

