## 5. Validation Methodology and Experimental Design

### 5.1 Phase 1 — Offline Benchmarking (Year 1, TRL 3→4)

The Hybrid PIML-DT is trained and validated against publicly available propulsion telemetry datasets—NASA P3 archives, university rocket lab databases, and ESA open datasets—supplemented by physics-based synthetic data generated through high-fidelity simulation with progressive fault injection using Generative Adversarial Networks calibrated against the physical simulator's known failure modes. Bayesian calibration convergence is assessed via posterior predictive check against held-out real telemetry. The heat flux prediction model with Neumann boundary conditions is validated against thermocouple-derived heat flux estimates from historical test data. The anomaly detection pipeline is evaluated on the same datasets using leave-one-campaign-out cross-validation, with adversarial fault injection providing coverage of rare and novel failure modes absent in the historical record. The CenEVT method is validated by comparing tail risk predictions from small pilot samples (50–200 observations) against full-dataset EVT fits.

Target metrics for Phase 1 passage: Hybrid PIML-DT state estimation RMSE below 5% on held-out telemetry; heat flux prediction error below 10%; anomaly detection novel fault recall above 85%; false positive rate below 10%; uncertainty quantification calibration error below 5% as measured by expected calibration error across confidence bins; CenEVT tail prediction within 15% of full-dataset EVT estimates from 200-sample pilot datasets.

### 5.2 Phase 2 — Hardware-in-the-Loop Emulation (Year 2, TRL 4→5)

The HIL testbed interfaces real Ground Support Equipment sensors and actuator control electronics with the software safety architecture running on target edge compute hardware—NVIDIA Jetson AGX for AI inference and an Intel FPGA for deterministic watchdog and interlock logic. Injected failure scenarios include network jitter, sensor dropout sequences of up to three simultaneous channels, spoofed sensor signals designed to defeat individual detection components, and synthetic latency injection that stress-tests the 50 ms inference budget under realistic embedded compute loads.

**Failure Diversity Evaluation:** A dedicated test campaign injects identical fault signatures simultaneously into all three N-version input streams to measure the common-cause failure fraction (β_CCF). The protocol iterates through a library of fault signatures covering sensor faults, actuator faults, and process faults. Any version pair exhibiting correlated failures above β_CCF = 0.05 triggers redesign of the shared specification or training data.

Target metrics for Phase 2 passage: end-to-end sensor-to-arbitration pipeline latency below 20 ms for high-confidence alerts; missing modality detection recovery above 90%; zero false negative rate on hardware-injected hard limit violations; watchdog response time below 5 ms from limit violation to actuator command; β_CCF ≤ 0.05 across all fault signature classes.

### 5.3 Phase 3 — Sub-Scale Static Fire Bench (Year 3–4, TRL 5→6)

Validation on a 1–10 kN class LOX/LCH₄ or LOX/RP-1 test article at an academic or industry partner facility constitutes the primary experimental contribution. Progressive anomaly injection—starting with single-modality instrumented faults and escalating to multi-modal cascade scenarios—tests the architecture against physical engine dynamics not representable in simulation. The autonomous shutdown logic is validated by real triggering events with post-test telemetry correlation confirming that the correct arbitration path was activated at the correct time. The runtime action-governance layer is validated by auditing the per-action oversight decision records for all autonomous interventions.

Target metrics for Phase 3 passage: 100% decision traceability—every arbitration decision linked to a cryptographically logged evidence chain including runtime governance records; safety case passing independent third-party review; zero uncontrolled cascade events during anomaly injection sequences; false positive abort rate below 10% across the full injection campaign; heat flux predictions validated against physical thermocouple measurements within 15%.

