### D.3 Hardware-in-the-Loop Testbed Configuration

The HIL testbed is organized into five physically separated racks interconnected via deterministic switched Ethernet (IEEE 802.1 Time-Sensitive Networking) with a programmable network impairment emulator on the inter-rack paths.

**Rack 1 — Sensor Simulation:** Real-time signal generators reproducing the full sensor suite at specified sampling rates. Supports: nominal signal playback from real telemetry archives, synthetic fault injection (amplitude anomaly, drift, spike, freeze, noise floor elevation), missing channel simulation (physical disconnection or software masking), censoring simulation (channel decimation, timeout injection, saturation), and spoofed signal injection via man-in-the-middle on the communication bus.

**Rack 2 — AI and Physics Compute (Under Test):** Production Jetson AGX Orin platform running the hybrid PIML residual network, DVAE-TDEN, GNN fusion, and V2 ensemble members under VxWorks 7. A separate Xeon node runs the reduced RANS baseline solver and V1 analytical models. Both nodes are instrumented with hardware performance counters monitoring CPU/GPU utilization, memory bandwidth, and inference latency at 1 ms resolution.

**Rack 3 — Watchdog and Interlocks:** Intel Agilex FPGA platform implementing the deterministic watchdog logic, actuator command relay simulation, and hardware interlock state machine. Instrumented for response time measurement with 10 ns resolution via oscilloscope trigger.

**Rack 4 — Governance and Audit:** ARM Cortex-R52 lockstep node running the runtime governance decision procedure and the hash-chained audit logger, with hardware root of trust for HMAC key storage. Equipped with a fault-injection interface for testing hash-chain integrity under power interruption.

**Rack 5 — Operator Interface and Physiology:** Simulation of the operator workstation display, cognitive load monitoring sensor inputs (via pre-recorded physiological signal playback), dual-authorization biometric emulator, and governance review interface with configurable timeout behaviour.

**Network Jitter Injection:** A programmable network impairment emulator introduces variable latency (0–50 ms, configurable distribution), packet loss (0–5%), and burst delay (0–200 ms) on inter-rack communication paths to stress-test the pipeline's real-time performance under realistic network degradation.

**Sandboxed Digital Twin Validation Environment:** A logically isolated compute instance hosting the hybrid PIML-DT under a frozen, versioned model artifact. All online adaptation events are mirrored into this sandbox, where the adapted model's predictions are compared against the certified baseline on a replay of the current sensor stream. Divergence between the sandbox-adapted and field-adapted models beyond a threshold triggers an adaptation-integrity alert — the mechanism by which continuous adaptation is kept within the certification basis established during offline validation.

